<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
  The launcher icon, from `brand/app-icon.png`. Regenerate with

      python3 scripts/generate-icons.py

  The master is a flat #5677DA tile with the white mark on it, so the two
  adaptive layers are exactly that: the tile becomes the background colour and
  the mark, lifted off it by coverage, becomes the foreground. The mark keeps
  the same share of the visible area it has in the master, so the master's
  padding is what decides how large the icon reads — see the script.

  The background is `launch_background` and not `brand_cornflower` because the
  master's tile is that colour. It also means the icon and the window the app
  opens on are the same blue, which is what the launch animation depends on.

  `monochrome` reuses the foreground: themed icons are drawn from alpha alone
  and the foreground has no colour of its own, only the mark's coverage.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/launch_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
</adaptive-icon>
